    "title": "Can You Bring a Box Cutter on a Plane? TSA Rules Explained",
    "description": "Can you bring a box cutter on a plane? Learn TSA rules for box cutters and utility knives in carry-on vs. checked baggage, and what happens at security.",
    "category": "Tools & Sharp Objects",
    "lastUpdated": "2026-08-10",
    "body": "[Box cutters](/items/utility-knife/) are a hard no in carry-on bags — full stop. There's no exception, no workaround based on [blade](/items/knife/) length, and no TSA officer discretion. If you're a contractor or warehouse worker who uses one every day, the rule is simple: it goes in your checked bag, or it doesn't fly with you at all.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## TSA Rules Summary\r\n\r\n- **Carry-On Baggage (Cabin):** ❌ Strictly Prohibited — No exceptions\r\n- **Checked Baggage (Hold):** ✅ Allowed\r\n- **TSA Declaration Required:** No (for checked baggage)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Official Regulations\r\n\r\n### Box Cutters in Carry-On: Absolutely Prohibited\r\n\r\nBox cutters, utility [knives](/items/knife/), and [razor](/items/razor/) blade-style cutting tools are **explicitly banned from carry-on baggage** by the TSA. This is one of the most firm, non-discretionary rules in airport security — there are no exceptions, no exemptions, and no workarounds.\r\n\r\nThe TSA's prohibited items list explicitly names:\r\n\r\n- Box cutters\r\n- Utility knives (e.g., Stanley knife, X-Acto knife)\r\n- [Razor blades](/items/razor-blades/) (not in a cartridge)\r\n- Any [knife](/items/knife/) with a retractable blade\r\n\r\n> [!IMPORTANT]\r\n> Box cutters were used as weapons in the September 11, 2001 terrorist attacks. As a result, they became one of the first items to be permanently and unconditionally banned from aircraft cabins. This rule will not change, and there are zero exceptions regardless of profession, employer, or intended use.\r\n\r\nIf a TSA officer finds a box cutter in your carry-on bag at the security checkpoint, it **will be confiscated immediately**. You will not be allowed to check it at the gate. You will either need to surrender it or go back and check your bag — and even that may not be possible at every airport.\r\n\r\n### Box Cutters in Checked Baggage: Allowed\r\n\r\nYou **can** pack a box cutter in your checked luggage. The TSA permits sharp and cutting tools in checked baggage with one requirement: the item must be **sheathed or securely wrapped** to prevent injury to baggage handlers.\r\n\r\nPractical packing tips for checked bags:\r\n- Put the box cutter in its original plastic housing with the blade retracted\r\n- [Wrap](/items/sandwich/) it in a sock, towel, or bubble wrap\r\n- Place it toward the center of your luggage (not near the zipper)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Box Cutter vs. Other Knife Types\r\n\r\n| Item | Carry-On | Checked |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Box cutter / [utility knife](/items/utility-knife/) | ❌ Prohibited | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| [Pocket knife](/items/pocket-knife/) (blade under 2.36 in) | ❌ Prohibited (US domestic) | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| [Swiss army knife](/items/multi-tool/) | ❌ Prohibited | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| Scissors (blades under 4 inches) | ✅ Allowed | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| [Disposable razor](/items/disposable-razor/) (cartridge) | ✅ Allowed | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| Razor blade (loose) | ❌ Prohibited | ✅ Allowed (sheathed) |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## International Rules for Box Cutters\r\n\r\nMost international aviation authorities maintain the same blank"
