    "title": "Can You Bring Candles on a Plane? TSA Rules for Wax & Scented Candles",
    "description": "Can you bring candles on a plane? TSA rules for wax candles, scented candles, and gel candles in carry-on and checked baggage fully explained.",
    "category": "Household Items",
    "lastUpdated": "2026-08-10",
    "body": "[Candles](/items/candles/) are one of those items where the type genuinely matters. A solid wax candle is no problem at security — TSA treats it like any other solid item. A gel candle, on the other hand, falls under liquid rules and needs to fit in your 3-1-1 bag if you're carrying it on. Here's the full breakdown.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## TSA Rules Summary\r\n\r\n- **Solid [Wax Candles](/items/candles/) in Carry-On:** ✅ Allowed\r\n- **Gel Candles in Carry-On:** ❌ Not Allowed (classified as gel/liquid)\r\n- **All Candles in Checked Baggage:** ✅ Allowed\r\n- **Candle Wicks / [Matches](/items/matches/) / [Lighter](/items/lighter/):** Separate rules apply\r\n- **TSA Declaration Required:** No\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Official Regulations\r\n\r\n### Solid Wax Candles\r\n\r\nStandard wax candles — whether paraffin, soy, beeswax, or coconut wax — are **solid items** and are not subject to the TSA's 3-1-1 liquids rule. You can pack solid wax candles in your carry-on bag without restriction.\r\n\r\nThis includes:\r\n- Pillar candles\r\n- Taper candles\r\n- Votive candles\r\n- Tea light candles (solid wax)\r\n- Soy or beeswax candles in jars (where the wax itself is solid)\r\n\r\n> [!IMPORTANT]\r\n> The key is that the wax must be fully solid. A candle that has been partially burned and has a pool of liquid wax around the wick may be treated differently by TSA officers.\r\n\r\n### Gel Candles\r\n\r\nGel candles — made from a transparent or translucent gel compound — are **classified as a gel/liquid by the TSA**. This means they fall under the 3-1-1 rule:\r\n\r\n- Must be in containers of **3.4 oz (100ml) or less**\r\n- Must fit in your **one quart-sized liquids bag**\r\n- Larger gel candles must go in **checked baggage**\r\n\r\nA large decorative gel candle (typically 8–16 oz) cannot go in your carry-on.\r\n\r\n### Candle Accessories\r\n\r\n| Item | Carry-On | Checked |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Wick trimmer (scissors-like) | ✅ If [blades](/items/knife/) under 4 inches | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| Matches | ✅ One book, on person only | ❌ Prohibited |\r\n| Lighter | ✅ One [disposable lighter](/items/lighter/) | ✅ With restrictions |\r\n| Candle snuffer | ✅ Allowed | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| Candle wax melts (solid) | ✅ Allowed | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| Liquid wax/lamp oil | ❌ Flammable — prohibited | ❌ Prohibited |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Practical Packing Tips for Candles\r\n\r\nCandles — especially glass-jarred premium candles — are fragile. Here's how to pack them safely:\r\n\r\n**For carry-on:**\r\n- [Wrap](/items/sandwich/) in bubble wrap or clothing\r\n- Place in the center of your bag away from hard edges\r\n- Keep away from sharp objects that could crack the jar\r\n\r\n**For checked baggage:**\r\n- Double-wrap in bubble wrap\r\n- Place inside a sealed plastic bag in case of leakage (if the wax were to melt in extreme cargo hold temperatures)\r\n- Mark the bag as fragile with the airline\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## International Travel with Candles\r\n\r\nCandles generally don't face customs restrictions internationally — they're not food, animal-based, or agricultural products (with the exception of some beeswax candles in certain c"
