    "slug": "perfume-on-plane",
    "title": "Can You Bring Perfume on a Plane? TSA Liquid Rules for Fragrances",
    "description": "Can you bring perfume on a plane? TSA carry-on limits for perfume, cologne, and body spray. How to pack fragrances safely in carry-on and checked luggage.",
    "category": "Liquids",
    "lastUpdated": "2026-08-10",
    "body": "[Perfume](/items/cologne/) gets confiscated at airport security more than almost any other personal care item. Not because it's restricted, but because standard [fragrance](/items/cologne/) bottles are well over the 3.4 oz (100 ml) carry-on liquid limit. Here's exactly how to fly with your fragrance without losing it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## TSA Rules Summary\r\n\r\n- **Perfume in Carry-On:** ⚠️ Restricted — 3.4 oz (100ml) per bottle maximum\r\n- **[Cologne](/items/cologne/) in Carry-On:** ⚠️ Restricted — 3.4 oz (100ml) per bottle maximum\r\n- **Perfume in Checked Baggage:** ✅ Allowed — with quantity limits for alcohol content\r\n- **Body Spray (Aerosol) in Carry-On:** ⚠️ 3.4 oz or less\r\n- **TSA Declaration Required:** No\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Official Regulations\r\n\r\n### Perfume Is a Liquid Under TSA Rules\r\n\r\nPerfume, [cologne](/items/perfume/), eau de toilette, eau de parfum, and body spray are all classified as **liquids** by the TSA. Every bottle of fragrance must comply with the 3-1-1 carry-on liquids rule:\r\n\r\n- Each perfume bottle must be **3.4 oz (100ml) or less**\r\n- All perfume bottles must fit in **one quart-sized clear zip-top plastic bag**\r\n- Only **one bag per passenger**\r\n\r\n### Common Perfume Bottle Sizes vs. TSA Rules\r\n\r\n| Bottle Size | TSA Carry-On Status |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| 5ml / 0.17 oz (travel atomizer) | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| 10ml / 0.34 oz | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| 30ml / 1 oz | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| 50ml / 1.7 oz | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| 75ml / 2.5 oz | ✅ Allowed |\r\n| **100ml / 3.4 oz** | ✅ Allowed (exactly at the limit) |\r\n| 125ml / 4.2 oz | ❌ Not allowed in carry-on |\r\n| 200ml / 6.7 oz | ❌ Not allowed in carry-on |\r\n| 500ml / 16.9 oz | ❌ Not allowed in carry-on |\r\n\r\n> [!IMPORTANT]\r\n> The standard large perfume bottle sold in most department stores is 100ml or larger. A 125ml bottle — even if it's 90% empty — cannot go in your carry-on. TSA measures the container size, not the remaining perfume.\r\n\r\n### Perfume in Checked Baggage\r\n\r\nPerfume and cologne can go in checked luggage, but with an important caveat: perfumes contain high percentages of alcohol, which classifies them as **flammable liquids** under DOT/FAA regulations.\r\n\r\nFAA rules for alcohol-based products in checked baggage:\r\n- **Up to 70% alcohol:** Limited to **68 oz (2 liters) total** per passenger\r\n- **Above 70% alcohol:** Prohibited in checked baggage\r\n- Most perfumes are 15–30% alcohol — well within the safe range\r\n- Standard perfume collections (several bottles) are typically under the 2-liter total limit\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## How to Travel With Perfume\r\n\r\n### Option 1: Travel Atomizer (Best for Carry-On)\r\nDecant your perfume into a refillable travel atomizer. These small spray bottles (typically 5–15ml) allow you to bring your favorite [scent](/items/perfume/) in carry-on without carrying the full bottle. Available on Amazon for $5–15.\r\n\r\n### Option 2: Travel-Sized Perfume (Pre-packaged)\r\nMany luxury fragrance houses (Chanel, Dior, Jo Malone, YSL) sell their fragrances in 10–30ml travel sizes. These fit easily in your carry-on liqu"
