    "category": "Travel Security",
    "lastUpdated": "2026-10-08",
    "body": "# 7 Secret TSA Checkpoint Exemptions Most Passengers Don't Know About\r\n\r\nNearly every flyer knows the basic drill: take off your shoes, remove your laptop, and pack all liquids into 3.4-ounce (100 ml) bottles inside a single quart-sized clear zip-top bag.\r\n\r\nWhat most travelers do *not* know is that federal aviation regulations contain multiple formal legal exemptions to the 3-1-1 liquids rule and standard screening requirements. Because these exceptions are not heavily advertised on airport signage, passengers frequently throw away expensive contact lens solution, baby formula, prescription liquids, and perishable medications unnecessarily.\r\n\r\nHere are the 7 official, legally binding TSA checkpoint exemptions that every traveler should know before packing their carry-on bags.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Medically Necessary Liquids Over 3.4 oz (100 ml)\r\n\r\nUnder **Title 49 CFR § 1544.219**, medically necessary liquids, gels, and aerosols are **completely exempt** from both the 3.4-ounce volume limit and the quart-sized bag requirement.\r\n\r\n### What Qualifies:\r\n* Prescription liquid medications, syrups, and inhalers.\r\n* Over-the-counter liquid medications (pain relievers, cough syrups, allergy liquid).\r\n* **[Contact Lens Solution](/items/contact-lens-solution/):** Even full-sized 12-ounce bottles of sterile saline and contact solution are classified as over-the-counter medical liquids.\r\n* Eye drops, ear drops, and nasal sprays.\r\n* Medically required liquid nutrition, electrolytes, and glucose gels.\r\n\r\n### How to Screen It:\r\nYou do not need to squeeze these items into your 3-1-1 quart bag. Before your bag enters the X-ray scanner, simply remove the medical liquids, inform the TSA officer that you are traveling with medically necessary liquids, and present them in a separate bin for visual and chemical vapor screening. A doctor's prescription note is helpful but not legally required under federal guidelines.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Baby Formula, Breast Milk & Toddler Juices\r\n\r\nParents traveling with infants and toddlers enjoy one of the broadest exemptions in commercial aviation.\r\n\r\n* **Formula and Breast Milk:** Permitted in \"reasonable quantities\" well exceeding 3.4 ounces.\r\n* **Child Presence Not Required:** You do **not** need to have the infant or child traveling with you to transport expressed breast milk or formula through the checkpoint.\r\n* **Purified Water for Mixing:** You are allowed to carry bottles of water through the checkpoint if they are specifically intended for mixing powdered infant formula.\r\n* **Purees and Teething Gel:** Baby food pouches, teething creams, and toddler smoothies are permitted in quantities suitable for the duration of travel.\r\n\r\nInform the TSA officer at the start of the lane. Officers will test the exterior of the bottles using vapor or liquid bottle scanners without touching or contaminating the liquid inside.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Ice Packs, Freezer Packs & Gel Packs for Medical & Nursing Needs\r\n\r\nNormally, liquid or gel freezer packs m"
