    "slug": "shampoo-bars-on-plane",
    "title": "Can You Bring Shampoo Bars on a Plane? TSA Rules Explained (2026)",
    "description": "Shampoo bars are solid — they are NOT subject to TSA liquid rules. Any size is allowed in carry-on with no restrictions. Heres the full guide to flying with solid shampoo.",
    "category": "Liquids",
    "lastUpdated": "2026-08-17",
    "body": "[Shampoo bars](/items/shampoo-bar/) are probably the best TSA-friendly swap for your hair routine. Because they're solid, they completely bypass the 3-1-1 liquids rule. Any size, any quantity, no liquid bag required.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## TSA Shampoo Bar Rules Summary\r\n\r\n- **Carry-On (Cabin):** ✅ Allowed — any size, no restrictions, no liquids bag needed\r\n- **Checked Baggage:** ✅ Allowed — no restrictions\r\n- **TSA Declaration Required:** No\r\n- **Goes in quart-sized bag?:** No — solid items are exempt from the liquids bag requirement\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Are Shampoo Bars Considered a Liquid by TSA?\r\n\r\n**No. Shampoo bars are solid and are NOT subject to the TSA 3-1-1 liquids rule.**\r\n\r\nTSA classifies items as liquids based on whether they can flow, pour, spread, or spray. A [solid shampoo](/items/shampoo-bar/) bar does none of these — it is physically a solid and is treated the same as bar soap.\r\n\r\n| Product Format | TSA Liquid Rule? | Carry-On Size Limit |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **Shampoo bar (solid)** | ❌ No | ✅ Any size |\r\n| **[Conditioner bar](/items/shampoo-bar/) (solid)** | ❌ No | ✅ Any size |\r\n| **Liquid shampoo (bottle)** | ✅ Yes | ⚠️ 3.4 oz max |\r\n| **Liquid [conditioner](/items/shampoo/) (bottle)** | ✅ Yes | ⚠️ 3.4 oz max |\r\n| **[Dry shampoo](/items/dry-shampoo/) (aerosol spray)** | ✅ Yes | ⚠️ 3.4 oz max |\r\n| **Dry shampoo (powder)** | ❌ No (powder) | ✅ Any size (over 12 oz may be screened) |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## The TSA Travel Advantage of Shampoo Bars\r\n\r\nThis is the #1 reason frequent travelers switch to shampoo bars:\r\n\r\n| Problem with Liquid Shampoo | Solution with Shampoo Bar |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| 3.4 oz limit forces travel sizes | No limit — full-size bar allowed |\r\n| Must go in quart-sized bag | No bag needed |\r\n| Risk of leaking in luggage | Solid — zero leak risk |\r\n| Adds to liquids bag space | Frees up your entire liquids bag for other items |\r\n| Must buy travel-sizes or decant | Use your regular bar directly |\r\n\r\nFor carry-on only travelers, shampoo bars are a game changer — they eliminate one of the biggest liquid packing constraints.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Popular Shampoo Bars: All TSA-Compliant\r\n\r\nAll of the following are solid bars and fully carry-on compliant in any size:\r\n\r\n| Brand | Product | Notes |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **Lush** | Shampoo Bars | Wide range, solid, highly popular for travel |\r\n| **HiBar** | Shampoo + [Conditioner](/items/conditioner/) Bars | Specifically designed for travel |\r\n| **Ethique** | Shampoo + Conditioner Bars | Zero-waste, compostable packaging |\r\n| **Kitsch** | Shampoo Bar | Color-safe, sulfate-free |\r\n| **Briogeo** | Don't Despair, Repair Shampoo Bar | Damage repair [formula](/items/formula/) |\r\n| **Unwrapped Life** | Various shampoo bars | Canadian brand, travel-friendly sizes |\r\n| **Beauty Kubes** | Shampoo Cubes | Cubed format, completely solid |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## How to Pack Shampoo Bars for Travel\r\n\r\n### 1. Use a Soap Case or Tin\r\nShampoo bars need somewhere to dry and stay contained. Options:\r\n- Metal tins with drainage holes\r\n- Silicone s"
