    "slug": "traveling-with-baby-formula",
    "title": "Traveling with Baby Formula & Breast Milk: TSA Rules (2026)",
    "description": "Complete guide to TSA rules for flying with baby formula, pumped breast milk, juice, baby food purees, cooling gel packs, and checkpoint screening procedures.",
    "category": "Baby",
    "lastUpdated": "2026-08-20",
    "body": "# Traveling with [Baby Formula](/items/baby-formula/), [Breast Milk](/items/baby-milk/) & Baby Food\r\n\r\nFlying with an infant is already a lot. TSA at least makes the [formula](/items/formula/) and [breast milk](/items/breast-milk/) part easy: both are classified as medically necessary liquids and explicitly exempt from the 3.4 oz (100 ml) liquid limit. Here's exactly how the exemption works and what to expect at the checkpoint.\r\n\r\nFlying with an infant or transporting nourishment for your baby can feel stressful, but TSA regulations explicitly recognize **[baby formula](/items/formula/), pumped breast milk, toddler drinks, and baby food as medically necessary liquids**. This classification exempts them from the standard 3.4 oz (100ml) liquid restriction.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## What Baby Nourishment Items Are Exempt from 3-1-1?\r\n\r\nThe following items are permitted in **reasonable quantities** in your carry-on luggage, even if individual containers exceed 3.4 ounces:\r\n\r\n* **Baby Formula:** Powdered formula (in canisters or dispensers) and ready-to-feed [liquid formula](/items/baby-formula/) bottles.\r\n* **Breast Milk:** Fresh or frozen pumped breast milk in bottles, bags, or storage containers.\r\n* **Baby Food & Purees:** Canned baby food, jars of puree, and squeeze [pouches](/items/baby-food/).\r\n* **Toddler Drinks & [Juice](/items/juice/):** Water, electrolyte solutions (e.g. Pedialyte), and [fruit juices](/items/juice/) intended for infant or toddler consumption.\r\n* **Cooling Accessories:** [Ice packs](/items/ice-pack/), frozen [gel packs](/items/ice-pack/), freezer inserts, and insulated coolers used to maintain milk and food freshness.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Do You Need to Be Traveling with Your Baby?\r\n\r\n**No.** TSA explicitly allows passengers to carry pumped breast milk, formula, and cooling accessories **regardless of whether an infant or toddler is traveling with them**. \r\n\r\nWhether traveling for business, returning from a solo trip, or moving milk to another location, your breast milk and formula enjoy the exact same exemptions.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Step-by-Step Checkpoint Screening Process\r\n\r\n1. **Separate Items:** Place all baby formula, breast milk, juice, baby food, and cooling packs in a separate bag or cooler bag inside or alongside your carry-on luggage.\r\n2. **Inform the Officer:** As you approach the security checkpoint conveyor [belt](/items/belt/), notify the TSA officer that you are carrying baby formula or breast milk.\r\n3. **Remove for Individual Screening:** Place the cooler bag or individual bottles in a separate screening bin.\r\n4. **Testing Methods:**\r\n   * **Bottle Liquid Scanners:** Officers will often place unopened or clear liquid bottles into an automated optical bottle scanner.\r\n   * **Vapor Strip Test:** If a container cannot be scanned with optical sensors, an officer may ask to open the bottle and hold a chemical test strip over the top of the container to test the air vapors. **Officers are not permitted to dip test strips directly into the mil"
